        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Alarcos</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">18 July 1195, a Wednesday</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Alarcos (al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arak-iran">Arak</a> in Arabic), near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a> River, the southern limit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile</a>, in what is now central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> army formed around a small hill called La Cabeza, two bow-shots from Alarcos</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Abu Yusuf Ya'qub <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-al-mansur">al-Mansur</a>, with his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Abu Yahya ibn Abi Hafs commanding the vanguard; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, about 8,000 strong, was led by Diego López de Haro</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> won; the Castilian field army was destroyed and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>. The garrison of the unfinished fortress of Alarcos surrendered on terms and many nearby castles fell or were abandoned.</span></div>
        </div>
