    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1191</span><span class="rf-text">Al-Mansur recaptures <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silves">Silves</a> from Portugal and returns to his capital, and an armistice with the Christian kings follows</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1194</span><span class="rf-text">At the end of the truce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso</a> invades southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, led by the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-calatrava">Order of Calatrava</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1195</span><span class="rf-text">Al-Mansur lands at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarifa">Tarifa</a> on 1 June and reaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> on 30 June</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1195</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> army crosses the Muradal pass and destroys a Christian cavalry detachment, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso</a> marches from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> to Alarcos</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1195</span><span class="rf-text">On Wednesday 18 July the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> defeat the Castilian army at Alarcos, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso</a> flees to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1195</span><span class="rf-text">Many nearby castles, among them Calatrava and Malagón, surrender or are abandoned, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-al-mansur">al-Mansur</a> takes the title al-Mansur Billah</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1198</span><span class="rf-text">Al-Mansur returns to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and he dies in February 1199</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1212</span><span class="rf-text">Al-Mansur's successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-nasir">Muhammad al-Nasir</a> is defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Las Navas de Tolosa</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-alarcos" data-v="d32b363645" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The two armies form up</b> The Almohads formed around the hill of La Cabeza in a vanguard and a rearguard. The Castilian king placed his heavy cavalry in one compact group and kept the infantry and Military Orders to follow.</li><li><b>The Castilian cavalry charges down</b> Alfonso chose to move first. His forces came down from their high position and the heavy cavalry charged across the lower plain into the front lines of the Muslims.</li><li><b>The Bani Marin and Zenatas are dispersed</b> In a three hour engagement the knights first dispersed the Bani Marin and the Zenatas, who held the first line of the Almohad vanguard.</li><li><b>The Hintata are attacked and the vizier killed</b> The knights then attacked the Hintata, the vizier's own guard behind the first line, and killed the vizier Abu Yahya, who commanded the Almohad vanguard.</li><li><b>The Andalusi forces are routed</b> The knights then struggled against the al-Andalus forces of Ibn Sanadid and routed them, but they were fatigued and men were falling.</li><li><b>The Almohad flank and rearguard surround the knights</b> As the Christians tired and fell, the Arab left under Yarmun enveloped their flank and rear. The Almohad rearguard under al-Mansur then attacked and surrounded the knights.</li><li><b>Alfonso commits the remaining forces</b> Alfonso advanced with all his remaining forces into the melee and was assaulted from all sides under a rain of arrows. For some time he fought hand-to-hand.</li><li><b>Alfonso is taken from the field</b> His bodyguard removed Alfonso from the action, almost by force, after he had fought in the melee, and together they fled to Toledo.</li><li><b>The infantry and the Orders are destroyed</b> With the ring closed round the knights, the Castilian infantry was destroyed, together with most of the Orders which had supported them.</li><li><b>López de Haro reaches Alarcos</b> López de Haro tried to force his way through the ring of enemy forces. He finally took refuge in the unfinished fortress of Alarcos with just a fraction of his knights.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii.jpg" alt="Alfonso VIII" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfons8_Tumbo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1190 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-al-mansur">al-Mansur</a> travelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> to invade Portugal, which had captured the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silves">Silves</a> the year before. After a campaign of about 15 months he recaptured the city in July 1191 and returned to his capital. An armistice between the Almohads and the Christian kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León followed.</p><p>When the truce expired in 1194, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> of Castile decided to invade Almohad territory in southern al-Andalus, including the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> River. He struck then because he had learned that al-Mansur was gravely ill and that the caliph's brother Abu Yahya, the governor of al-Andalus, had gone to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> to claim the Almohad throne for himself. The invasion was led for Alfonso by Martín López de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisuerga">Pisuerga</a>, the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, supported by the military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-calatrava">Order of Calatrava</a>. In Marrakesh al-Mansur recovered, reasserted his control over his brother and began to plan an expedition into al-Andalus against the Christian invaders.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Alarcos&#8221; cites where it covers Background:</p><ul><li>Stephen Lay, The Reconquest Kings of Portugal: Political and Cultural Reorientation on the Medieval Frontier (Palgrave Macmillan, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon.jpg" alt="Alfonso IX of León" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon">Alfonso IX of León</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adeffonsus,_king_of_Galicia_and_Leon_(detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 1 June 1195 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-al-mansur">al-Mansur</a> landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarifa">Tarifa</a>. His main army passed through the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> on 30 June, reinforced by the troops of local governors and by a Christian cavalry contingent under Pedro Fernández de Castro, who held a personal feud against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso</a>. On 4 July al-Mansur left Córdoba and went through the Muradal pass into the Campo de Calatrava, the fertile plain dominated by the fortress of Salvatierra. On the way his forces intercepted and surrounded a Christian cavalry detachment that was tracking the Muslim army, and only a handful of scouts escaped to report to Alfonso.</p><p>Alfonso gathered his forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and marched to Alarcos, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a>, which marked the southern limit of his kingdom and where a fortress was under construction. He meant to bar access to the rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a> valley, and he did not wait for the reinforcements on their way from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon">Alfonso IX of León</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-vii-of-navarre">Sancho VII of Navarre</a>. He put most of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, about 8,000 strong, in a compact group under Diego López de Haro, lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Vizcaya</a>, and he himself was to follow with the infantry and the Military Orders. Alfonso had badly underestimated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> host.</p><p>Al-Mansur gave his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, Abu Yahya ibn Abi Hafs, command of a strong vanguard. In its first line were the Bani Marin volunteers under Abu Jalil Mahyu ibn Abi Bakr, with many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, and the Zenata tribe. Behind them stood the vizier with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a>'s banner and his personal guard, from the Hintata tribe. The Arab hosts under Yarmun ibn Riyah were on the left and the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> under the Caid Ibn Sanadid on the right. Al-Mansur held the rearguard himself, the best Almohad forces, with the black African guard.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> host came into view on 16 July, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-al-mansur">al-Mansur</a> did not accept battle at once, preferring to rest his forces. On Wednesday 18 July the Almohad army formed for battle around a small hill called La Cabeza, two bow-shots from Alarcos. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso</a> chose to move first and ordered his forces down from their high position, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> charging across the lower plain.</p><p>At the outset the battle went well for the Christians. After striking the Muslim front lines, the knights began a three-hour engagement in which they first dispersed the Bani Marin and the Zenata, then attacked the Hintata and killed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Abu Yahya, and finally fought against the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> under Ibn Sanadid and routed them. As the Christians fought, tired and fell, the Arab left under Yarmun enveloped their flank and rear. The Almohad rearguard under al-Mansur then attacked and surrounded the Christian knights.</p><p>Alfonso advanced with all his remaining forces into the melee and found himself assaulted from all sides under a rain of arrows. He fought hand to hand for some time until his bodyguard removed him from the action, almost by force, and together they fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>. The Castilian infantry was destroyed, together with most of the Orders that had supported it. The lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Vizcaya</a> tried to force his way through the ring of enemies but had to take refuge in the unfinished fortress of Alarcos with only a fraction of his knights.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The castle was surrounded, with some 3,000 people trapped inside, half of them women and children. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> sent Pedro Fernández de Castro, who had taken little part in the fighting, to negotiate the surrender. López de Haro and the survivors were allowed to go, leaving 12 knights as hostages for the payment of a great ransom.</p><p>The Castilian field army had been destroyed. The dead included three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avila">Ávila</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segovia">Segovia</a> and Sigüenza; Count Ordoño García de Roda and his brothers; Counts Pedro Ruiz de Guzmán and Rodrigo Sánchez; and the Masters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-santiago">Order of Santiago</a>, Sancho Fernández de Lemus, and of the Portuguese Order of St Benedict, Gonçalo Viegas. Muslim losses included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, and Abu Jalil Mahyu ibn Abi Bakr, commander of the Bani Marin volunteers, who died of his wounds in the following year. Al-Mansur also suffered considerable losses.</p><p>The outcome shook the stability of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> for several years. All the nearby castles surrendered or were abandoned, among them Malagón, Benavente, Calatrava, Caracuel and Torre de Guadalferza, which left the passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> wide open. Al-Mansur went back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> to make good his losses and took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-al-mansur">al-Mansur</a> Billah, 'The one victorious by God'. For two years his forces devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/extremadura">Extremadura</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a> valley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mancha">La Mancha</a> and the country around Toledo, and moved against Montánchez, Trujillo, Plasencia, Talavera, Escalona and Maqueda; some of these expeditions were led by Pedro Fernández de Castro. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> diplomacy won an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon">Alfonso IX of León</a>, who had been enraged that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> did not wait for him, and the neutrality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>. Both arrangements proved temporary. In 1198 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, in poor health, returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and he died in February 1199.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> order of battle is known commander by commander and tribe by tribe, and the Christian dead are known by name and title. Of the armies' strengths only two figures stand: the 8,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> under López de Haro and the 3,000 people in the castle.</p><p>What followed is told in two ways. The raids after the battle are said to have brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> no territorial gains, and it is also said that the Almohads reconquered Trujillo, Montánchez and Talavera. The commander of the Bani Marin volunteers is said to have died of his wounds a year later.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-nasir"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-nasir.jpg" alt="Muhammad al-Nasir" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-nasir">Muhammad al-Nasir</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_coin_of_Almohad_ruler_Abu_%27Abd_Allah_Muhammad_(r._1199-1213).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LouisAragon, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The success of the battle proved short-lived. The raids that followed brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> no territorial gains, the alliances his diplomacy won with León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> lasted only a time, and he lost interest in the affairs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>. His aim of keeping a hold over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> appeared to be a complete success when he left in 1198.</p><p>Sixteen years after Alarcos the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-nasir">Muhammad al-Nasir</a> tried to build on it with a new Iberian offensive and was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a> in 1212. That battle was a turning point that led to the end of Moorish rule in the Iberian Peninsula. The Almohad Caliphate itself collapsed a few decades later.</p><p>For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> the immediate legacy was the loss of its southern frontier. Alarcos had marked the southern limit of the kingdom, and the surrender or abandonment of Malagón, Benavente, Calatrava, Caracuel and Torre de Guadalferza left the passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> wide open, while the raids of the following two years reached the country around Toledo itself. The defeat also strained relations among the Christian kingdoms: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon">Alfonso IX of León</a>, enraged that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> had not waited for him, allied with the Almohads for a time, and Navarre stayed neutral. Al-Mansur, by contrast, took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-al-mansur">al-Mansur</a> Billah after the victory.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Alarcos on 18 July 1195 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-al-mansur">al-Mansur</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, destroyed his field army and left the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> open. The gains were brief, and the Almohads were beaten at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Las Navas de Tolosa</a> in 1212.</em></p>

